// Lesson 2.3 — Healing Pickups & the Max-Health Cap
// 3-way comparative analysis: Coin vs Spike vs Potion.
// Multiple functions for bounding values (if vs Mathf.Min vs Mathf.Clamp).
// GROUND TRUTH: there is no Potion prefab, no Potion tag, and no potion sprite
// anywhere in the project. The student creates all three, tinting an existing
// sprite (greyboxing). Health and the cap live on PlayerController.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.3 — Healing Pickups &amp; the Max-Health Cap</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You can gain wealth and lose health. Now you need a way to get health back — and the
    moment you add healing, you create a new kind of problem. Healing without a limit is not
    a feature, it is an exploit. Today you compare the potion against both things you have
    already built, then discover that a value with a ceiling needs a <em>rule</em>, not just
    arithmetic.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Comparative Thinking</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Good engineers do not design from nothing. When you meet a new mechanic, ask what you
        have already built that behaves like it:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Analysis</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion like the coin?</strong></td>
            <td>Both are <strong>pickups</strong>: trigger colliders, handled by <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code>, and destroyed on contact so they cannot be taken twice. Almost all of your coin code is reusable.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion like the spike?</strong></td>
            <td>Both change <strong>health</strong> — and health lives on <code>PlayerController</code>, exactly where your coin count lives.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion different from both?</strong></td>
            <td>It raises a value that now has a <strong>ceiling</strong>. Without a rule, ten potions means ten times the health, and the game stops being a game.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>There is no potion art in this project — you are going to make one.</strong>
        There is no Potion prefab, no Potion tag, and no potion sprite in
        <code>Assets/Artwork</code>. So: pick a sprite you <em>do</em> have, and tint it with the
        Sprite Renderer's <strong>Color</strong> field so it cannot be mistaken for a coin.
        Create the tag yourself. Building a mechanic with borrowed art is called
        <strong>greyboxing</strong>, and it is what professionals do all day — the mechanic is
        the work; the art is a placeholder until someone draws the real thing.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Similar, Different, and Why It Matters</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The potion reuses the coin's trigger, its callback, and its destroy. What is the <em>only</em> thing that genuinely differs?</li>
            <li>Without a ceiling, a player who collects ten potions has ten times the health. Which breaks first — the challenge, the economy, or the feedback the player gets?</li>
            <li>The rule "health must never exceed the maximum" has to live <em>somewhere</em>. Name two places it could live, and say which one keeps the rule true no matter which script calls the healing method.</li>
            <li>If a second healing source arrives in Unit 5 — a heart, a shrine, a level-up — is a ceiling check inside the <em>potion</em> script still enough? What does your answer say about where rules belong?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Four ways to keep a number inside its limits, each from a different system. Read them in
        isolation and work out what each one can and cannot do:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A water reservoir dam, using <code>if</code>:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>if (currentGallons &gt; maxReservoirCapacity)
{
    currentGallons = maxReservoirCapacity;
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — An electric scooter battery, using <code>Mathf.Min</code>:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Mathf.Min returns the smaller of the two, so the battery cannot charge past 100%
batteryLevel = Mathf.Min(batteryLevel + chargeAdded, 100f);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — A spaceship thruster, using <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Mathf.Clamp bounds a value at BOTH ends at once - here, 0 and 100
thrusterPower = Mathf.Clamp(requestedThrust, 0, 100);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A reservoir overflow alarm, an <code>if</code> with a side effect:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>if (currentGallons &gt;= maxReservoirCapacity)
{
    Debug.Log("Overflow: intake refused.");
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><code>currentGallons</code> is 95 and 20 gallons arrive. Trace Example 1: what is the value before the check, inside the body, and after it?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between <code>Mathf.Min</code> and <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>? In a game where damage can drive health down as well as up, which one covers both ends?</li>
            <li>Example 4 logs a refusal. Which of the first three examples could produce that message on its own — and why can the other two not?</li>
            <li>If you clamp health at 0, have you also implemented death? What is the difference between "this value cannot go below zero" and "the player dies"?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: The Potion</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the prefab.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>In the Hierarchy, right-click → <strong>2D Object → Sprite</strong>. Name it <strong>Potion</strong>, then drag it into <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> so it becomes reusable. What is the difference between the object in the scene and the asset in the folder?</li>
            <li>Which sprite will you use, and what Color tint makes it obviously <em>not</em> a coin at a glance?</li>
            <li>Add a collider. Trigger or solid — and what is your reason? Which of the two things you built this unit does this make it most like?</li>
            <li>You are about to create a tag that does not exist in this project. Where does the tag list actually live? Does the <em>prefab</em> need the tag, or does one instance in the scene?</li>
            <li>Before you write <code>CompareTag("Potion")</code> in code, how do you check the tag exists? What happens if you get the spelling wrong?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the rule.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>What field holds the maximum health? How is it different from the current-health field, and which of the two should be the designer's tuning knob?</li>
            <li>Where exactly does the ceiling get applied — before the heal, after the heal, or <em>inside</em> the method that changes health? Which of those cannot be bypassed by a future caller?</li>
            <li>Pick one of the three capping approaches from the examples. What does your choice do that the other two cannot?</li>
            <li>If you chose <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>, what is the <em>lower</em> bound — and what does clamping at 0 mean for the death check you wrote in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the trigger.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which callback, and which tag check?</li>
            <li>Which method on the player do you call, and do you pass a positive or a negative number?</li>
            <li>Do you destroy the potion? Which object — <code>other.gameObject</code> or <code>gameObject</code>?</li>
            <li>What should happen when a player already at full health touches a potion: consume it, or leave it on the ground? Both are defensible. Pick one, and name the extra check your choice requires and where that check belongs.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save, press Play, and verify. Drop your health on a spike, then walk into a potion and
        confirm it comes back up. Then walk into another potion at full health and confirm your
        rule holds.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt: Boundary Conditions</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set maximum health to <code>5</code> in the Inspector. Does the code still cap correctly, or did you hardcode <code>3</code> somewhere in your file?</li>
            <li>Change your cap comparison from <code>&gt;</code> to <code>&gt;=</code>, or the reverse. With health <em>exactly</em> at the maximum, what happens now — and how would you ever have noticed if you only ever tested from the middle?</li>
            <li>Forget to create the tag, then call <code>CompareTag("Potion")</code> anyway. What does Unity report? Why is a loud error better here than silence?</li>
            <li>Make the potion solid instead of a trigger. What does the player do when they walk into it, and does the heal still fire?</li>
            <li>Heal at full health under each of the three capping approaches. Which one silently does nothing, and which one could tell you the heal was wasted?</li>
            <li>Set maximum health <em>below</em> your current health while the game is running. What does your rule do — and is that a bug or a feature?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Testing Mathf.Clamp</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Rewrite your ceiling using <code>Mathf.Clamp(health, 0, maxHealth)</code>. Test both spikes and potions. Does one line replace two separate checks — and what did you have to be careful about?</li>
            <li>Implement your answer to "should a full-health player waste a potion?" Which check did you add, and where did you put it?</li>
            <li>Read the docs for <code>Mathf.Min</code> and <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>. Which would you use if a designer said "health may go negative — that is how we count overkill damage"?</li>
            <li>Where else in this game will a value need a ceiling — a fire rate, a detection radius, a jump count, a coin total? Which of the three approaches would you reach for there, and is it the same one you chose here?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain how the potion is like the coin, like the spike, and unlike both</li>
            <li>Create a prefab and a brand-new project tag from scratch</li>
            <li>Explain the overheal exploit, and which selection rule prevents it</li>
            <li>Compare <code>if</code>, <code>Mathf.Min</code> and <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>, and defend the one you chose</li>
            <li>Explain why the rule lives in the method that changes health rather than in the potion</li>
            <li>Test at the boundary — health exactly at maximum — instead of only in the middle</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — selection, pickups and health in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the reference for trigger pickups</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Mathf and selection syntax</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.4.html">2.4 — Prefabs, Spawning &amp; Playtesting</a>, where one working object becomes a whole level.</p>
</div>`;
